    "comment_count": 1,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author": {
                "avatarUrl": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/24854248?u=d09fd974c6720eba3039db6beb2f1c1efecdb51d&v=4",
                "login": "echasnovski",
                "resourcePath": "/echasnovski",
                "url": "https://github.com/echasnovski"
            },
            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the suggestion!\r\n\r\n'mini.cursorword' will keep highlighting only based on the text (word under cursor, a.k.a. `<cword>`).\r\n\r\nWhat you are asking for is not really achievable with tree-sitter (as it does not really keep connections between nodes, only their structure). It *is* doable with LSP., though. On Neovim 0.10 there is a `vim.lsp.buf.document_highlight()` function which highlights based on the current position with the help of LSP server. See `:h vim.lsp.buf.document_highlight()` for the basic setup it can provide.",
            "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">Thanks for the suggestion!</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">'mini.cursorword' will keep highlighting only based on the text (word under cursor, a.k.a. <code class=\"notranslate\">&lt;cword&gt;</code>).</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">What you are asking for is not really achievable with tree-sitter (as it does not really keep connections between nodes, only their structure). It <em>is</em> doable with LSP., though. On Neovim 0.10 there is a <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.lsp.buf.document_highlight()</code> function which highlights based on the current position with the help of LSP server. See <code class=\"notranslate\">:h vim.lsp.buf.document_highlight()</code> for the basic setup it can provide.</p>",
            "bodyText": "Thanks for the suggestion!\n'mini.cursorword' will keep highlighting only based on the text (word under cursor, a.k.a. <cword>).\nWhat you are asking for is not really achievable with tree-sitter (as it does not really keep connections between nodes, only their structure). It is doable with LSP., though. On Neovim 0.10 there is a vim.lsp.buf.document_highlight() function which highlights based on the current position with the help of LSP server. See :h vim.lsp.buf.document_highlight() for the basic setup it can provide.",
